{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The Nephilim</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 6:4): they were so named because they fell (נפלו) and caused the world to fall. And this is in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 26:7). The masters of the language (Ibn Ezra and Radak) said that they were so called because a person's heart falls (יִפֹּל) upon him from dread of them, and similarly the Emim (Genesis 14:5)." "<b>In those days</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 6:4): in the days of the generation of Enosh. \"And also after that\" — they saw [what happened] in the destruction of the generation of Enosh, when Oceanus rose and flooded a third of the world, yet they were not humbled to learn from them. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained [that it means] also after the Flood, for the sons of Anak were of the family of the sons of Elohim. And if so, either the wives of the sons of Noah were of their seed and resembled them, or he concedes to the statement (Niddah 61) that expounds concerning Og that he escaped from the Flood, and he [further] adds that others were saved together with him.\n\nBut the correct interpretation in my eyes is that Adam and his wife were called \"sons of Elohim,\" because they were the work of His hands and He was their father — they have no father other than Him — and he begot many children, as it is written (Genesis 5:4), \"and he begot sons and daughters.\" And these men, those born first from a father and mother, were of great perfection in height and strength, for they were born in the likeness of their father, as it is written concerning Seth (Genesis 5:3), \"and he begot in his likeness, after his image.\" And it is possible that all the primordial sons — Adam, Seth, Enosh — were called \"sons of Elohim,\" for these three men were in the image of Elohim. Then idolatry began to be practiced, and weakness and frailty began to enter mankind. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 24:6): \"This is the book of the generations of Adam\" — the earlier ones were not [called] \"generations,\" and what are they? Deities! In the name of Abba Kohen Bardela: Adam, Seth, Enosh — and he was silent. He said to them: Up to here, in image and likeness; from here onward, Cain, the quarrelsome ones. And when man began to multiply and daughters were born to them, these — the sons of the first ones — in their strength and abundance of desire, would choose the best women, those of tall stature and healthy. And Scripture stated at the beginning (Genesis 6:2) that they would take them as wives by way of violence, and afterward it stated that they would come by way of harlotry to the daughters of men who were not of that caliber, and the matter would not be known until they begot sons by them and they recognized that they were not like the rest of men, but rather were born to these sons of Elohim, who were exceedingly large, though they fall short of their fathers in height and strength, in the language of \"I am not inferior to you\" (Job 12:3). And they were mighty compared to the rest of mankind. And he said that this was in the first generations, who were called \"sons of Elohim\" when they were at the height of perfection, that they would beget Nephilim from the daughters of men, \"and also after that,\" for the Nephilim themselves would beget Nephilim from among themselves. And the meaning of \"who were of old\" is that the men after the Flood, when they see mighty men, will recall these and say that there were already mighty men from of old, who were before us, and they were \"men of renown\" in all the generations thereafter. And this is a proper plain sense in this passage.\n\nBut the midrash of Rabbi Eliezer the Great in his chapters, concerning the angels who fell from their holy place from heaven, which is mentioned in the Gemara in tractate Yoma (Yoma 67), is more fitting to the language of Scripture than all [the others]. Except that there is need to elaborate at length on the secret of that matter:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "The Aramaic/Hebrew transitional passage citing Bereshit Rabbah 24:6 includes the phrase 'בְּעוּן קוּמֵי אַבָּא כֹּהֵן בַּרְדְּלָא' ('in the name of Abba Kohen Bardela') and 'קֵינָן קַנְטְרָנִין' (Aramaic: 'Cain, the quarrelsome ones'); rendered literally preserving the code-switching."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:25:38.476203194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}